<h2>Contents Claims After a Fire</h2>
<p>The personal property part of a fire claim often takes the most time. Many policies require an itemized inventory with the description, age, and replacement cost of each item. Photos and video taken before cleanup, purchase records, and room-by-room lists help. Under replacement cost contents coverage, insurers often pay depreciated value first and the rest after items are replaced. Watch for special limits on jewelry, firearms, collectibles, and business property kept at home. Items that can be cleaned are often sent to a contents restoration company; items that cannot be restored should be listed for replacement.</p>

<h2>Rebuilding and Building Codes</h2>
<p>Major fire repairs in Dallas generally require permits through the City of Dallas Planning &amp; Development Department, and the work must meet the city&rsquo;s adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code. Cities such as Richardson, Garland, Irving, and Plano have their own permit offices. Upgrades required by code are paid only if your policy includes ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> for more.</p>

<h2>Common Fire Claim Disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Scopes that clean surfaces that should be replaced, or limit work to the burned room.</li>
<li>Contents valued with heavy depreciation or missing items.</li>
<li>ALE limited in time or amount before repairs are realistically finished.</li>
<li>Disagreements over whether a structure is repairable or a total loss.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the insurer&rsquo;s numbers fall short, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may help resolve a disagreement over the amount of loss.</p>

<h2>Fires at Businesses and Rental Properties</h2>
<p>Commercial fires raise additional questions. A restaurant kitchen fire, a warehouse electrical fire, or a fire in one apartment unit can shut down operations, damage tenant improvements, and spread smoke to neighboring spaces. Business income and extra expense coverage, if purchased, may help with lost revenue and continuing costs during restoration, usually subject to a waiting period and the policy&rsquo;s definitions. Landlords may also have loss of rents coverage. Keep financial records, payroll reports, and lease documents together from the start. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> or <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> for more on each.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, represents policyholders on fire losses throughout Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. We can document the building with photos and Matterport 3D imaging, prepare an Xactimate estimate, help organize the contents inventory, track living expenses, and communicate with your insurer. We do not perform repairs on claims we adjust, and we cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>First Steps After a Fire</h2>
<ol>
<li>Do not enter the building until the fire department says it is safe.</li>
<li>Get a copy of the fire department report or incident number.</li>
<li>Report the claim and ask about emergency living expenses.</li>
<li>Secure the property with board-up and tarps, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph everything before cleanup or demolition.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the insurer raises questions about the cause of the fire or denies the claim, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
